/* client_architecture section 10: safe-area driven, no fixed pixel positions.
 *
 * --safe-top/--safe-bottom are declared here as the documented default (0)
 * for whatever renders before the first frame. UI Toolkit's runtime style API
 * has no public way to SET a custom property from script (only to read one
 * that a stylesheet already defines - UnityEngine.UIElements.CustomStyleAccess
 * only exposes TryGetValue, verified against UnityEngine.UIElementsModule.dll
 * for this Editor version), so BootController does not actually write through
 * these variables. It computes the safe-area inset from Screen.safeArea via
 * RuntimePanelUtils.ScreenToPanel and applies it as concrete inline padding,
 * which wins the cascade over the rule below regardless. These two lines stay
 * as the spec'd fallback and as documentation of the intent.
 *
 * ON THE PANEL ROOT, NOT ON #shell-root - THIS PARAGRAPH SAID #shell-root AND
 * IT WAS FALSE. BootController.BindSafeAreas pads `document.rootVisualElement`,
 * which is the PARENT of both #shell-root and #notice-layer. The distinction is
 * not pedantry: #shell-root is an in-flow child and is pushed down by that
 * padding, while #notice-layer is `position: absolute` below and UI Toolkit
 * offsets an absolutely positioned child from its parent's border box rather
 * than its padding box - so the layer and everything in it sat OUTSIDE the
 * inset, which is how the notice toast came to draw under an iPhone 17's
 * Dynamic Island. BindSafeAreas pads the toast itself for that reason and its
 * header carries the whole account.
 */
:root {
    --safe-top: 0;
    --safe-bottom: 0;
}

.shell-root {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-direction: column;
    padding-top: var(--safe-top);
    padding-bottom: var(--safe-bottom);
}

.top-bar {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.screen-host {
    flex-grow: 1;
}

/* `bottom: 0` IS #shell-root's BOTTOM, WHICH IS BELOW THE TAB BAR, AND THAT
 * IS DELIBERATE - Phase 9 Task 21i. A bottom sheet is flush with the bottom of
 * the frame; it does not stop at a piece of chrome. What it costs is that the
 * tab bar and every sheet occupy the same band, and for four tasks this file
 * declared the layer BEFORE `#tab-bar`, so the bar won that band twice over.
 *
 * MEASURED, AT 402x874 WITH THE IPHONE 17'S OWN 62/34 INSETS, by
 * `probe-shell.sh` against this very .uxml:
 *
 *     #shell-root   y=  62.0  h=778.0  bottom=840.0
 *     #screen-host  y=  78.0  h=706.0  bottom=784.0   clear of the bar
 *     #tab-bar      y= 784.0  h= 56.0  bottom=840.0
 *     #sheet-layer  y=  62.0  h=778.0  bottom=840.0   spans the bar entirely
 *     forfeit       y= 745.0  h= 55.0  bottom=800.0   16.0 under the bar
 *
 * and `IPanel.Pick` down the button's own centre column answered `TabBar` for
 * every point from y=784 to y=799. `OnTabSelected` is empty, so those taps were
 * delivered, swallowed and answered with nothing - which is what a player met
 * on an iPhone 17: the lower third of the only button on `AbandonedWaveSheet`
 * did not work, four times. `CodexSheet`'s dismiss was worse, 28 points of 49.
 *
 * THE ORDER IN Shell.uxml IS THE FIX AND THE ONLY FIX, because UI Toolkit has
 * no z-index: both paint order and hit-test order are sibling order, so the
 * layer that is meant to be on top has to be declared last. Nothing here moved
 * and nothing about the tab bar moved - an absolutely positioned child is out
 * of flow, so its position among its siblings changes no box's geometry.
 *
 * WHAT IT CHANGES FOR THE PLAYER, said plainly because it is a behaviour change
 * and not only a repair: while a sheet is up, its scrim now covers the tab bar
 * and takes taps there. That is what a modal overlay is for, it is what the
 * scrim was already claiming visually, and `ScreenHost.ShowSheet`'s "the tab
 * bar's visibility does not change under it" still holds - the bar is still
 * displayed, now dimmed under the scrim rather than drawn on top of it.
 *
 * `#notice-layer` NEEDED NONE OF THIS and the difference is worth keeping: it
 * is `PickingMode.Ignore` from its root down (see below, and `NoticeToast.cs`),
 * so it has always overlapped everything and stolen nothing. A sheet cannot
 * take that route - it exists to be pressed. */
.sheet-layer {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    display: none;
}

/* Sibling of #shell-root, not a child: Task 6 hides the shell while a wave
 * is resident, and a notice must still reach the player then. Picking is
 * ignored so the layer never swallows a tap; only its rows are visible. */
.notice-layer {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
}

.tab-bar-slot {
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.tab-bar {
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: space-around;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- chrome
 *
 * Added with the design pass. The rules above this line are layout and
 * safe-area only - deliberately, per client_architecture section 10 - and
 * nothing below changes a position or a flex value.
 *
 * The bottom nav follows the handoff's navigation model verbatim: active tab
 * is a violet-tint pill with violet-text at the heavier weight, inactive is
 * mute. The handoff specifies five tabs (Map / Ark / Splice / Lab / Allies)
 * with 19px 1.9-stroke icons; this phase draws text-only tabs and only the
 * ones Progression.TabsFor unlocks, so the icon half is not implemented and
 * the pill/colour half is.
 */

.top-bar {
    background-color: var(--paper);
    padding-top: var(--space-2);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2);
    padding-left: 14px;
    padding-right: 14px;
    align-items: center;
}

/* NO PADDING ON THE SLOT, AND THAT IS A DECISION MADE ONCE FOR ALL TEN
 * SCREENS. This rule used to carry `padding-left`/`padding-right:
 * var(--gutter)`, and every screen the slot has ever held ALSO carries a
 * 12px gutter of its own - the seven that still pad their own root, and the
 * three that hand it to `.screen-scaffold__content`. So the shipped app drew
 * a 24px gutter everywhere the handoff asks for 12, and it drew a screen
 * header meant to sit flush at the frame's edge 12px in from it.
 *
 * MEASURED IN A PICTURE, NOT READ OUT OF TWO STYLESHEETS. It survived this
 * long because `ScreenshotCapture` hosted every fixture in a bare
 * VisualElement, so no rule of this file reached the corpus at all; that
 * harness now builds the slot, and the capture immediately before this
 * commit has RosterView's grid and CampaignSelectView's first row both
 * starting at x=24. After it, both start at x=12.
 *
 * THE FRAME KEEPS THE GUTTER, NOT THE SLOT. A frame that carries its own
 * works in any container; a slot that pads its contents works only for
 * things that do not pad themselves, which is not what any of the ten are.
 * Nothing else is affected: `WaveHudView` never reaches `ScreenHost` at all
 * (`WaveRunner` adds it to the wave scene's own panel root) and `CodexSheet`
 * goes to `#sheet-layer` through `ScreenHost.ShowSheet`. Checked, not
 * assumed - `ScreenHost.SetScreen` is the only `Add` into this element.
 *
 * So there is no `.screen-host` rule in the chrome section of this file, and
 * the layout one above the chrome line - `flex-grow: 1` - is the whole of
 * what the slot does. StatCell.uss's closing note is the same shape of
 * record: the absence is the decision. */

.tab-bar {
    background-color: var(--surface);
    border-top-width: 1px;
    border-top-color: var(--divider);
    padding-top: var(--space-2);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2);
    padding-left: var(--space-2);
    padding-right: var(--space-2);
}

/* A tab is a Button, so it inherits the primary-CTA fill from Theme.uss.
 * All of that is undone here: a nav item is not a call to action, and the
 * drop edge in particular would read as five buttons stacked on a shelf. */
.tab-bar__tab {
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    color: var(--mute);
    font-size: var(--text-nav);
    border-bottom-width: 0;
    /* Half of a MEASURED 39px, rounded down. Was var(--radius-pill), which
     * drew each 83x39 tab as an ellipse 41px wide and 19px tall - a lens
     * where the handoff wants a pill, on the one piece of chrome that is on
     * screen at all times. Theme.uss header item 4 has the measurement. */
    border-radius: 19px;

    /* THE GLYPH'S BAND, AND THE ONLY REASON THIS IS NOT var(--space-2).
     * A Button is a TextElement: it draws `text` ITSELF, centred in its own
     * content box, and a child element is not a flex sibling of that text -
     * so the icon Phase 8 Task 4 inserts lands ON TOP of the first letters
     * of the word unless something moves the text out from under it. The
     * text centres in the content box, which is what is left after this
     * padding, so reserving the top 31px pushes the label below the band and
     * `icons.uss` pins the mark absolutely into it. 8 + 19 + 4: the
     * handoff's nav padding, its 19px glyph, its 4px gap.
     *
     * NOT SOLVED BY MOVING THE LABEL INTO A CHILD `Label`, which would make
     * glyph and label real flex siblings and is how the handoff's own HTML
     * does it - `TabBarTests.Render_DrawsOneButtonPerTabInOrder` asserts on
     * `((Button)c).text`, and emptying it to satisfy the layout would delete
     * a gate to make a picture nicer. */
    padding-top: 31px;
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2);
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    padding-right: var(--space-4);
    margin: 0;
    flex-grow: 1;
}

.tab-bar__tab:active {
    translate: 0 0;
    background-color: var(--surface-sunk);
}

.tab-bar__tab--active {
    background-color: var(--violet-tint);
    color: var(--violet-text);
}

.tab-bar__tab--active:active {
    background-color: var(--violet-tint);
}
